Systematically, or just to generate the seed.  I don't have
/dev/srandom on my machines, just /dev/random and /dev/urandom;
I use /dev/random to seed my generators, but it's too slow to be
used in game.  (You get the lot of bytes very quickly, but after
that, it slows down enormously.)

>  > If any game is suited for a
>  > cryptographically secure random number generator,
>  > it's Nethack ;-)

> Chuckle.

> That's a nice answer for *nix play, but not a general
> solution, while so far as I know, the MT is such a
> solution for any platform where C compiles.

    FILE* devrand =3D fopen( "/dev/random", "rb" ) ;
    if ( devrand !=3D NULL ) {
        /* use it for seed */
    } else {
        /* use something else */
    }

Using this to get the seed for an MT generater would probably be
the best solution.

    [...]
> It's also interesting to read from the Wikipedia page that the
> MT is not in the least bit cryptographically secure.

That's a separate issue.  In the case of nethack, I don't think
that cryptographically secure is very relevant: you don't have
direct access to any of the output of the generator anyway, to
begin trying to crack it.

(Interesting thought, however: if you know what generator was
being used, it might be possible to write a bot which cracked
it, and was then able to know in advance what the program was
going to throw at it.)

